    {
        "content": "<p>Thank you! And yes, good suggestion <span aria-label=\"+1\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f44d\" role=\"img\" title=\"+1\">:+1:</span></p>",
        "id": 609033728,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Butcher",
        "timestamp": 1783529237
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Great point of view of Lean as a software programming language. <br>\nAt the beginning i would emphasize the <code>function</code> to <code>proofs</code> ratio is about 1 function for around 10 to 20 proofs/lemmas.  That's a rough estimation, it can be much more or a litle less depending of the function and it's complexity.<br>\nSo the engineering cost is not the same as usual software (even with AI, because the token cost can explode).</p>\n<p>At the end, i would not cite the \"HTTPS server\" as fully verified. I looked into it and it's mostly AI slop with no value. I think it would be better to cite the <a href=\"https://github.com/pandaman64/lean-regex\">regex library</a> as a fully verified one, and this one is a real gem of carefully crafted proof for software engineering.<br>\nAnother one is kim fully autoformalized <a href=\"https://github.com/kim-em/lean-zip\">zip library</a> verified implementation correctly using AI.</p>",
        "id": 609038483,
        "sender_full_name": "Alfredo Moreira-Rosa",
        "timestamp": 1783530708
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thank you: I was in no position to have a meaningful opinion on it. I will make that change.</p>",
        "id": 609038684,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Butcher",
        "timestamp": 1783530763
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't think this line:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong><code>#guard</code> runs at compile time</strong>. So these tests will run (and succeed or fail) without having to be compiled and then run; just compiling is enough.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>is correct. This command both compiles and evaluates.</p>\n<p>I think it is also worth pointing out where the proofs generated by Claude are not great. As one example, there are some odd usages of <code>.induct</code> where <code>fun_induction</code> would be much nicer. In general, I strongly disagree with the notion that </p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>We don't need to understand [the proofs] in depth. We just need to know that the theorems we care about are true. In this, we're different from mathematicians.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>is true or one of the differences between formalizing mathematics and software verification at scale.</p>",
        "id": 609039087,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1783530900
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Agree that the proofs generated by Claude aren't great. And there will certainly be occasions where the quality of the proof is essential (where the proof is associated with a widely used library, or where understanding is an important part of what we're generating the proof for: e.g. it's part of CSLib).</p>\n<p>What issues might I hit in the future if I don't spend time cleaning up Claude's mess?</p>",
        "id": 609039768,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Butcher",
        "timestamp": 1783531127
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I was mostly thinking of maintenance for larger developments, as both performance and readability can be poor.</p>",
        "id": 609040533,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1783531370
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm planning to have a crack at something much larger, so it will be interesting to see if/when I hit a wall <span aria-label=\"stuck out tongue wink\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f61c\" role=\"img\" title=\"stuck out tongue wink\">:stuck_out_tongue_wink:</span></p>",
        "id": 609041118,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Butcher",
        "timestamp": 1783531562
    }
]